{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>To work it and to keep it.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): since the Holy One, blessed be He, had already enumerated his food — seeds and fruits — He now placed him in the Garden of Eden so that he would work the soil of the Garden and sow for himself there every kind of grain and plant for himself there every kind of fruit; for this was why he was placed there: to delight and enjoy himself as he wished. His sustenance was from the trees of the Garden, his drink from the streams of Eden, and his garment clouds of glory. His station there was in exceedingly great and mighty delight — much in the delight of the soul and little in the delight of the body — in tranquility and repose and contentment such as has no equal, his entire being being intellectual: his soul and body were one in apprehending his Creator alone. And therefore the Tree of Knowledge, which causes attraction after desires, was withheld from him, whereas the Tree of Life, which brings eternal life, was not withheld from him at first. But after he sinned and ate from the Tree of Knowledge, that eating caused him to be drawn after desires and to occupy himself with the needs of the body more than the needs of the soul, just as everything we do here today is the opposite of what is sought from us — and therefore he was punished that his days be shortened and death rule over him." "<b>And you should</b> know that to measure the delight of man in the Garden of the world to come, when the world will return to the resurrection of the dead: the children of the resurrection will delight, body and soul alike, yet the greater part will be in the delight of the soul and the lesser in the delight of the body, like the first man before he sinned. And therefore he is bound to live a long life or an eternal life, for then the world will return to its perfection as it was. And the meaning of לעבדה ולשמרה will return to the soil of the Garden." "<b>By way of the Midrash:</b> לעבדה — what work was there inside the Garden that it says לעבדה? Should you say to prune vineyards and to plow and harrow the soil, or to reap or to thresh? Behold, all the trees sprout on their own. Should you say there is labor in the Garden of Eden? Behold, a river flows and goes out from Eden, as it says ״ונהר יוצא מעדן להשקות את הגן״ — so what is לעבדה? Rather: to engage in words of Torah, and לשמרה — to keep the entire commandment, as it says ״לשמור את דרך עץ החיים״, and עץ החיים is nothing but Torah, as it says (Proverbs 3:18) ״עץ חיים היא למחזיקים בה״. לעבדה ולשמרה — this is the Sabbath: לעבדה, as it says ״ששת ימים תעבוד״, and לשמרה, as it is written ״שמור את יום השבת״. לעבדה ולשמרה — these are the offerings: לעבדה, as it is written (Exodus 3:12) ״תעבדון את האלהים על ההר הזה״, and לשמרה, as it is written (Numbers 28:2) ״תשמרו להקריב לי במועדו״." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah:</b> לעבדה ולשמרה — one must contemplate these two letter הי״ן, for it should have said לעבדו ולשמרו, since הגן (the Garden) is masculine, as in what is written (Song of Songs 4:12) ״גן נעול״. But these הי״ן allude to the two letters ה in the Name: לעבדה — the first ה in the Name, and לשמרה — the last ה in the Name. And concerning this it says (Exodus 23:21) ״השמר מפניו״, and the discerning one will understand."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:09:31.063526507Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}